This page is part of an opening 'formulaire' on Weierstrass equations for genus-1 curves with a chosen invariant differential pi, over an arbitrary base. It sets up the material needed later to compute the Gauss–Manin connection and Picard–Fuchs equations.

The general Weierstrass equation is F(x,y) = y^2 + a1xy + a3y - x^3 - a2x^2 - a4x - a6 = 0, with pi = dx/F_y. Page 4 defines b2, b4, b6, b8 and two further quantities d6, d8. This page continues as follows.

(1.5) gives the standard Tate quantities, with Deligne's sign convention for c6:
- c4 = b2^2 - 24 b4;
- -c6 = b2^3 - 36 b2 b4 + 216 b6;
- Delta = -b2^2 b8 - 8 b4^3 - 27 b6^2 + 9 b2 b4 b6.

It adds an alternative form, Delta = b4^3 - 27 b6^2 + b8(36 b4 - b2^2). This follows from the first form using the relation 4 b8 = b2 b6 - b4^2: the difference of the two expressions is -9 b4 (b4^2 - b2 b6 + 4 b8) = 0.

(1.6) Under the change of coordinates (1.2), x = u^2 x' + r and y = u^3 y' + s u^2 x' + t (with pi' tied to pi by a power of u), it gives Tate's transformation laws for a1, ..., a6. These are written compactly through the values of F and its partials at the point (r,t):
- u^3 a3' = F_y(r,t);
- u^6 a6' = -F(r,t);
- u^4 a4' = -F_x(r,t) - s F_y(r,t).
In this notation F_t means the partial derivative in the second variable.

(1.7) gives the b-transformation laws, which depend only on r. Each new b is a Taylor-type polynomial in r:
- 6r^2 + b2 r + b4 has discriminant c4;
- 4r^3 + b2 r^2 + 2 b4 r + b6 has discriminant 16 Delta (it is the 2-division polynomial).

The non-standard combinations d6 = b2 b4 - 18 b6 and d8 = b2^2 b4 - 48 b4^2 + 18 b2 b6 transform affinely in r:
- u^6 d6' = d6 + c4 r;
- u^8 d8' = d8 - c6 r.
This mirrors the fact that b2/12 shifts by r, exactly like the quasi-modular E2 (P).

Page 4 records 12 d6 = c6 + b2 c4 and -12 d8 = c4^2 + b2 c6. These are the Ramanujan/Serre-derivative relations: d6 and d8 are, up to normalisation, the derivatives of c4 and c6 under the Gauss–Manin/Ramanujan derivation, with b2 playing the role of E2. This prepares the computation of the Gauss–Manin connection in the coordinates (g2, g3) or (c4, c6). The next page draws the conclusions u^4 c4' = c4, u^6 c6' = c6, u^12 Delta' = Delta, i.e. c4 pi^4, c6 pi^6 and Delta pi^12 are canonical sections of omega^{4,6,12}.
